\uline{Remark} When $\pi\neq(1)$, \inserted{that is, $K$ not finite over $\mathbb Q$,} theorem 1 can be strengthened, \inserted{unless I am mistaken,} by establishing that for \unsure{every|every} subgroup \inserted{$\pi'\subset\pi$} open \uline{in $\pi$}, $\operatorname{Centr}_{\unsure{\mathcal E|E}}(\pi')=\{1\}$. If $\unsure{Z|\mathcal Z}$ is this centralizer, we have $\unsure{Z|\mathcal Z}\cap\pi=(1)$ by theorem 1, \deleted{and}\inserted{let us prove that} the image of $\unsure{Z|\mathcal Z}$ in $\Gamma_{\bar K/K}\subset\Gamma_{\overline{\mathbb Q}/\mathbb Q}$ is \deleted{contained in the \unsure{kernel|kernel}}\inserted{finite} (which will \inserted{already} then prove, that $\unsure{Z|\mathcal Z}$ is of order $1$ or $2$, and in the second case that its image in $\Gamma_{\overline{\mathbb Q}/\mathbb Q}$ is \deleted{an \unsure{element}} generated by a complex conjugation $\tau$) --- Replacing $\unsure{\mathcal E|E}$ by a sufficiently small open subgroup if necessary (which amounts to passing to a finite extension of $K$) we may assume $\pi'=\pi$, then the image $\unsure{Z'|\mathcal Z'}$ of $\unsure{Z|\mathcal Z}$ in $\Gamma$ is contained in the kernel of the homomorphism $\varphi:\Gamma\longrightarrow\operatorname{Aut\,ext}(\pi)$. Now (unless I am mistaken) I know how to prove that this homomorphism is injective (we are immediately reduced to the case where $K$ has transcendence degree $1$, and we are reduced to the case \inserted{of the $\pi_1$} of an algebraic curve\ldots)
